, Y., and Y. Murata. <year>2001</year>. Control of rectification and permeation by two distinct sites after the second transmembrane region in Kir2.1 K<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> channel. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>531</volume>:<fpage>645</fpage>&#x02013;660.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11251047</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal">Kuo, A., J.M. Gulbis, J.F. Antcliff, T. Rahman, E.D. Lowe, J. Zimmer, J. Cuthbertson, F.M. Ashcroft, T. Ezaki, and D.A. Doyle. <year>2003</year>. Crystal structure of the potassium channel KirBac1.1 in the closed state. <source>Science.</source> <volume>300</volume>:<fpage>1922</fpage>&#x02013;1926.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12738871</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Kurata, H.T., L.R. Phillips, T. Rose, G. Loussouarn, S. Herlitze, H. Fritzenschaft, D. Enkvetchakul, C.G. Nichols, and T. Baukrowitz. <year>2004</year>. Molecular basis of inward rectification: polyamine interaction sites located by combined channel and ligand mutagenesis. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>124</volume>:<fpage>541</fpage>&#x02013;554.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15477380</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Kurata, H.T., L.J. Marton, and C.G. Nichols. <year>2006</year>. The polyamine binding site in inward rectifier K<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>127</volume>:<fpage>467</fpage>&#x02013;480.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16606689</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Lopatin, A.N., E.N. Makhina, and C.G. Nichols. <year>1994</year>. Potassium channel block by cytoplasmic polyamines as the mechanism of intrinsic rectification. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>372</volume>:<fpage>366</fpage>&#x02013;369.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7969496</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Lopatin, A.N., E.N. Makhina, and C.G. Nichols. <year>1995